OL,3,ANational museums to stay free for all
OL,4,Avisitors
OL,5,
OL,6,FOverseas visitors will not be charged
OL,7,Fto enter England's national museums
OL,8,Fafter ministers decided against
OL,9,Fchanging the universal free admission
OL,10,Fpolicy.
OL,12,FIt comes after months of discussions
OL,13,Fbetween the government, museums, the
OL,14,Ftourism sector and local leaders to
OL,15,Fexamine whether making tourists from
OL,16,Fabroad pay could boost the
OL,17,Finstitutions' finances.
OL,19,FIn March, the government said it would
OL,20,Fexplore charging international visitors
OL,21,Fto enter national museums as it looks
OL,22,Ffor ways to tackle funding pressures.
OL,23,APub: 29-SepQpppppppppppppppppppppG10/19
OL,24,ANext PageBCultureC Nat RadioFTechnology
FL,162,160,200,105,0,100
PN,11911
SC,0011
CT,25,T
PS,c000
OL,0,        ECIMSBWebfax 2   F119A1790700494
OL,1,W5j 5Q7+j 55"k#j 5w;js1
OL,2,W//%*// //%Q-.*,%-,* *,%%**,$ ,,,,,,,,,,
OL,3,ANational museums to stay free for all
OL,4,Avisitors
OL,5,
OL,6,FBut the Department for Digital,
OL,7,FCulture, Media and Sport (DCMS) has now
OL,8,Fsaid this could deter domestic visitors
OL,9,Fand that any benefits of charging would
OL,10,Fnot be felt equally across the sector.
OL,23,APub: 29-SepQpppppppppppppppppppppG11/19
